'''time-sense''' (tīm′sens), ''n.'' The sense or perception of time and time-relations.

{{smaller block|All psychophysic experiments, especially those requiring comparison and those upon the ''time-sense'' and the like, involve memory.
{{right|''W. H. Burnham'', Amer. Jour. Psychol., II. 603.}}}}

'''time-server''' (tīm′sėr″vėr), ''n.'' One who acts conformably to times and seasons: now generally applied to one who meanly and for selfish ends adapts his opinions and manners to the times; one who panders to the ruling power.

{{smaller block|No government has ever been, or ever can be, wherein ''timeservers'' and blockheads will not be uppermost.
{{right|''Dryden'', Third Miscellany, Ded.}}}}

{{smaller|='''Syn.''' See definitions of ''temporizer'' and ''trimmer.''}}

'''time-serving''' (tīm′sėr″ving), ''n.'' An acting conformably to times and seasons; now, usually, an obsequious compliance with the humors of men in power, which implies a surrender of one's independence, and sometimes of one's integrity.

{{smaller block|By impudence and ''time-serving'' let them climb up to advancement in despite of virtue.
{{right|''Burton'', Anat. of Mel., p. 375.}}}}

